    <h2 id="faqs" class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">8. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div class="space-y-4">
      <div>
        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">Is rental income taxable in Singapore?</h3>
        <p>Yes. Net rental income earned from leasing real estate properties or individual rooms in Singapore is fully taxable under personal income tax for individuals, or corporate tax for companies.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">What is the 15% deemed expense option?</h3>
        <p>It is a simplified scheme that allows residential landlords to deduct a flat 15% of gross rent, plus actual housing loan interest, without the need to keep individual receipts for maintenance and repairs.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">Is room rental income taxable in Singapore?</h3>
        <p>Yes. Rent received from leasing out bedrooms in HDB flats or private residential properties is considered taxable income and must be declared in your annual tax return.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">Can I deduct my property tax and fire insurance premiums?</h3>
        <p>Yes, property tax and fire insurance premiums are considered allowable expenses if you choose to claim actual rental expenses instead of utilizing the 15% deemed expense option.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">What happens if I forget to declare my rental income?</h3>
        <p>Failure to declare rental income can result in penalties, audits, and potential legal action by IRAS. It is vital to file accurately and on time.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

